{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"lambdas","title":"Lambda Expressions","group":"Functions","order":16,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A lambda is an unnamed function object written inline: <code>[captures](params) { body }</code>. It's how you pass behaviour to algorithms, threads and callbacks.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Syntax</h3>\n<div class=\"formula\"><code>[capture](parameters) mutable noexcept -&gt; ReturnType { body }</code></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    auto add = [](int a, int b) { return a + b; };       // return type deduced\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; add(2, 3) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{5, 2, 8, 1};\n    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int a, int b) { return a &gt; b; });   // descending\n    for (int x : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    int threshold = 3;\n    auto big = std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [threshold](int x) { return x &gt; threshold; });\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; big &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5\n8 5 2 1\n2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hand-grab\"></i>Captures</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Capture</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>[]</code></td><td>Nothing captured</td></tr><tr><td><code>[x]</code></td><td><code>x</code> by value (a copy taken when the lambda is created)</td></tr><tr><td><code>[&amp;x]</code></td><td><code>x</code> by reference</td></tr><tr><td><code>[=]</code></td><td>Everything used, by value</td></tr><tr><td><code>[&amp;]</code></td><td>Everything used, by reference</td></tr><tr><td><code>[=, &amp;x]</code> / <code>[&amp;, x]</code></td><td>Default plus exceptions</td></tr><tr><td><code>[this]</code> / <code>[*this]</code></td><td>The enclosing object by pointer / by copy (C++17)</td></tr><tr><td><code>[y = expr]</code></td><td>Init-capture (C++14): new variable, e.g. <code>[p = std::move(ptr)]</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    int n = 1;\n    auto byVal = [n] { return n; };      // copies n now\n    auto byRef = [&amp;n] { return n; };     // refers to n\n    n = 10;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; byVal() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; byRef() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto counter = [count = 0]() mutable { return ++count; };   // own state; `mutable` allows changing copies\n    counter(); counter();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; counter() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 10\n3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Capturing by reference (<code>[&amp;]</code>) in a lambda that outlives the scope — stored in a <code>std::function</code>, passed to a thread, returned from a function — leaves dangling references. Capture by value (or move) for anything that escapes.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>Generic and immediately-invoked lambdas</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;functional&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nint main() {\n    auto twice = [](auto x) { return x + x; };               // generic lambda (C++14)\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; twice(21) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; twice(std::string(\"ab\")) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    const int config = [] {                                  // IIFE: initialise a const in steps\n        int v = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 4; ++i) v += i;\n        return v;\n    }();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; config &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::function&lt;int(int)&gt; fib = [&amp;fib](int k) {            // recursive lambda via std::function\n        return k &lt; 2 ? k : fib(k - 1) + fib(k - 2);\n    };\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; fib(10) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>42 abab\n10\n55</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Recursion</div><p>Recursive lambdas: C++23 <em>deducing this</em> (<code>[](this auto self, int k)</code>) is the clean way; in C++20 use <code>std::function</code> (as above) or pass the lambda to itself as an <code>auto&amp;</code> parameter.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>What a lambda really is</h3>\n<p>The compiler turns each lambda into an unnamed class with an <code>operator()</code> — captured variables become data members. Captureless lambdas also convert to plain function pointers.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct AddN {                       // roughly what [n](int x) { return x + n; } becomes\n    int n;\n    int operator()(int x) const { return x + n; }\n};\nint main() {\n    int n = 5;\n    auto lambda = [n](int x) { return x + n; };\n    AddN functor{n};\n    int (*fp)(int) = [](int x) { return x * 2; };    // captureless → function pointer\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; lambda(1) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; functor(1) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; fp(4) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6 6 8</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does a by-value capture need mutable to be modified?</summary><div><p>The generated <code>operator()</code> is <code>const</code> by default, so captured copies are read-only inside the body. <code>mutable</code> removes that <code>const</code>, letting the lambda change <em>its own copies</em> (never the originals).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the type of a lambda?</summary><div><p>A unique, unnamed closure type generated by the compiler — two identical-looking lambdas have different types. That's why you store them with <code>auto</code>, pass them as template parameters, or wrap them in <code>std::function</code> when you need one uniform type.</p></div></details></section>"}